# Program system repository

This is the program's system repository. The technical program manager
owns it. It holds the program's intent: the vision, the architecture, the
release road map, and the top of the planning hierarchy. The work that
delivers that intent happens in the teams' subsystem repositories, which
point back here. A program of one team can be a program of one
repository: when control/trackers.md names a tracker_branch of this
repository, this repository is the system and its only component, and the
tracker lives on that branch, which is never merged.

## Read it in this order

1. docs/VISION.md: what the program is for, and what it is not.
2. docs/specs/themes/: the strategic areas the program serves.
3. docs/specs/initiatives/: the outcomes the program commits to, each under a theme.
4. docs/specs/epics/: the large bodies of work that deliver an initiative and span teams.
5. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md: the subsystems and how they fit together.
6. docs/engineering/: how the system is built, in the detail the architecture has no room for.
7. docs/road-map.md: the releases, and what each one proves.

Each folder under docs/specs/, and docs/engineering/, has a README that says what its documents are.

## The planning hierarchy

| Level | What it is | Timeframe | Owner | Parent | Lives in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Theme | A strategic area: the why | Months to years | Technical program manager | none | this repository |
| Initiative | An outcome several teams deliver: the program's Product Goal | Several quarters | Technical program manager | a theme | this repository |
| Epic | A large body of work under an initiative | One to three months | A Product Owner | an initiative | here when it spans teams, else the team's repository |
| Feature (optional) | A capability inside an epic | Two to four sprints | A Product Owner | an epic | the team's repository |
| Story | The unit of value a team finishes in one sprint | One sprint | The team's Product Owner | a feature or an epic | the team's repository |

The chain from intent to evidence runs initiative, epic, story, issue,
merge, release. Progress is computed from the story points of done
stories, so no one types a percentage. Each sprint, every team gets a
Sprint Goal issue that cites the initiative items it serves.

## The process

One release cycle, and the skill that serves each step:

1. Plan the next release, its items, and its dated period (the plan and plan-push skills).
2. File one Sprint Goal per team, citing the initiative items it serves (the tasks skill).
3. Draft the epics, features, stories, and requirements (the draft skill).
4. At Sprint Planning, choose the stories that fit the team's velocity (the plan skill).
5. Break each story into issues (the tasks skill).
6. Take, work, review, and merge each issue (the specify, pop, implement, and merge skills).
7. Release a version behind its gate.
8. Close the period against the Sprint Goals' ledgers (the sprint-end skill).

The Sprint Goal is the ledger: planned, evidence, met. The check skill is
the repository's check, the one CI runs.

Reviewers approve a branch with the approve skill and a release with the
peer-read skill; the release skill gates and tags an artifact. The status,
status-issues, and aggregate skills run the weekly status. The admit,
recur, comment, close, labels, and tracker-sync skills keep issues and the
tracker in order; tracker-migrate and tracker-export move the tracker to
one file per issue and write it out for bd. issue-file files any issue,
a recurring one included, and the model skills name a model-free profile
for each of their words, for an agent working without a model; doctor
repairs the drift the check skill reports.

What exists, what is being built, and what is planned is in
docs/architecture-status.md, which the architecture-status skill
regenerates: each ARCHITECTURE component actual as of the latest release,
in transition through the agreed documents that cite it in their
architecture item, or intended with no plan yet. ARCHITECTURE is the
intended state and each release tag the actual one, so the plan is the
difference between them, as the plan-vs-architecture guide of the
release docs/external-pins.yaml pins explains.

